          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched do not contain a full definition of osteoarthritis, so I can't fully explain what it is from them. What they do show:\n\n- **It is classified separately from other types of arthritis.** In the ICD-10-CM coding system (Chapter 13, musculoskeletal conditions), osteoarthritis has its own block, M15 to M19, and is sorted by joint, then by cause and side of the body. Rheumatoid arthritis and gout are classified elsewhere, in M05 to M14, as inflammatory conditions. So \"arthritis\" is a broad umbrella, and osteoarthritis is one specific type within it.\n- **It is not the same as inflammatory arthritis.** The osteoarthritis reference note contrasts the two. Long morning stiffness, several small joints affected on both sides of the body, or whole-body symptoms suggest inflammatory arthritis instead.\n\nThe same reference note says a hot, swollen, very painful joint with fever needs urgent assessment, because it may be infection or crystal arthritis rather than osteoarthritis. Sudden locking or giving way of a joint, or new nerve symptoms with back pain, also need prompt assessment.\n\nFor general guidance, the osteoarthritis guideline in this corpus describes exercise as the first step, with strengthening, movement and aerobic activity combined. Medicines are described as add-ons to exercise. For a full explanation of the condition itself, a clinician or a dedicated patient resource would be the best source.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
